|~ What is declared under `::quote`, the kind of code held as a value:
   its constructor and its refusals, the refusal of text read as code
   and the facts it carries, and the words that read a quote. A quote is
   also the vector of its steps, so the words of vectors read it as one,
   and a quote under a tag, a link or a snippet of a page, reads them
   through its shape. ~|

:quote/construct
  |~~ The quote of a vector whose every element is a step. ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:quote/PayloadNotVecError
  |~~ `::quote` builds code from the vector of its steps; a payload of
      another kind is refused.
      ::link~(::quote | doc)
  ~~|

:quote/PayloadNotVecError/refusesAMap
  |~~ A map payload is refused. ~~|
  ::law~(::quote{:not :steps} !| [type /actualType] | eq [::quote/PayloadNotVecError ::map])

:quote/ElementNotStepError
  |~~ Every element of a quote is a step: a literal, a quote, a
      vector, a set or a map of steps, or a step as data, `::call`,
      `::proj` and their kin. A value with no reading as code, a value
      under a tag of another kind among them, is refused at the index it
      stands at.
      ::link~(::quote | doc)
  ~~|

:quote/ElementNotStepError/refusesAValueWithNoReading
  |~~ A value with no reading as a step is refused at its index. ~~|
  ::law~([::Box[1]] | tag ::quote !| [type /index] | eq [::quote/ElementNotStepError 0])

:quote/ParseError
  |~~ The source does not read as qlang: `:marker` points where the
      reading stopped, `:expected` lists what could stand there, and a
      source cut short names the bracket it left open.
      ::link~(::quote | doc)
  ~~|

:quote/ParseError/source
  |~~ The line of the text where the reading stopped. ~~|
  ::string

:quote/ParseError/marker
  |~~ A caret under the place it stopped. ~~|
  ::string

:quote/ParseError/message
  |~~ What the grammar says of the place, a bracket left open among it. ~~|
  ::string

:quote/ParseError/expected
  |~~ What the reading could take there. ~~|
  ::vec

:quote/ParseError/found
  |~~ What it found there. ~~|
  ::string

:quote/ParseError/location
  |~~ The place, its offsets, lines and columns. ~~|
  ::map

:quote/ParseError/uri
  |~~ The source the text came from. ~~|
  ::string

:quote/ParseError/refusesASourceCutShort
  |~~ A source cut short after a pipe is refused. ~~|
  ::law~("42 |" | parse !| type | eq ::quote/ParseError)

:quote/parse
  |~~ Answers the source of the quote, a string, `~(add 1 | mul 2) |
      parse` answering `"add 1 | mul 2"`: the reverse of `parse` on a
      string, which reads text as a quote, so the source parsed again is
      the quote, `~(add 1) | parse | parse` answering `~(add 1)`. The
      source is printed in one spelling, so the spaces and the line
      breaks the quote was written with are not kept, `~(add   1) |
      parse` answering `"add 1"`. A link or a snippet out of a page
      answers the query it holds, without its tags, and the empty quote
      the empty string.
      ::link~(::string/parse | doc) ::link~(::quote/open | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:quote/parse/answer
  |~~ The text the quote reads back from. ~~|
  ::string

:quote/parse/answersTheSource
  |~~ A quote parses to its source. ~~|
  ::law~(~(add 1) | parse | eq "add 1")

:quote/parse/readsBackAsTheQuote
  |~~ The source parsed again is the quote. ~~|
  ::law~(~(add 1 | mul 2) | :q / | q | parse | parse | eq q)

:quote/parse/printsOneSpelling
  |~~ The spaces the quote was written with are not kept. ~~|
  ::law~(~(add   1 |   mul 2) | parse | eq "add 1 | mul 2")

:quote/parse/leavesTheTagsOfALink
  |~~ A link answers the query it holds, without its tags. ~~|
  ::law~(::link~(1 | add 1) | parse | eq "1 | add 1")

:quote/parse/answersNothingForTheEmptyQuote
  |~~ The empty quote parses to the empty string. ~~|
  ::law~(~() | parse | eq "")

:quote/open
  |~~ Answers what the query the quote holds answers, run beneath
      whatever tags stand over it, `~(1 | add 1) | open` answering `2`:
      a link opens to the page it names, so `links | map ~open` reads every
      page a page names in one query, and a snippet to the answer it
      shows. The query runs in the scope of the reader, so a name bound
      before it is read, `:k 5 | ~(k) | open` answering `5`, and the
      names it binds stay inside it. It runs from null, not from the
      subject of `open`: a query that reads a subject, `~(add 1)`, is
      refused on null, and a quote meant for a subject runs against it
      by `apply`, `5 | apply ~(add 1)`. Text is read as a quote first by
      `parse`, `"1 | add 1" | parse | open`.
      ::link~(::any/apply | doc) ::link~(::doc/links | doc)
      ::link~(::doc/snippets | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:quote/open/answer
  |~~ What the query answers, a value of any kind. ~~|
  ::any

:quote/open/SubjectNotQuoteError
  |~~ `open` runs a quote, `~(1 | add 1) | open`: text is read as a
      quote first by `parse`, `"1 | add 1" | parse | open`, and the links
      of a page are read by `links`, `::vec | doc | links | map ~open`.
  ~~|

:quote/open/runsALink
  |~~ A link opens to the answer of its query. ~~|
  ::law~(|~~ ::link~(1 | add 1) ~~| | links | first | open | eq 2)

:quote/open/runsASnippet
  |~~ A snippet opens to what it shows. ~~|
  ::law~(|~~ ::snippet~([3 1 2] | sort) ~~| | snippets | first | open | eq [1 2 3])

:quote/open/readsTheScopeOfTheReader
  |~~ A name bound before the quote is read inside it. ~~|
  ::law~(:k 5 | ~(k) | open | eq 5)

:quote/open/keepsItsNamesInside
  |~~ A name the query binds stays inside it. ~~|
  ::law~(:k 5 | ~(:k 1) | open | k | eq 5)

:quote/open/runsFromNull
  |~~ The query runs from null. ~~|
  ::law~(~(type) | open | eq ::null)

:quote/open/runsTextReadByParse
  |~~ Text read as a quote by `parse` opens. ~~|
  ::law~("1 | add 1" | parse | open | eq 2)

:quote/open/SubjectNotQuoteError/refusesAString
  |~~ Text is no quote until `parse` reads it. ~~|
  ::law~("1 | add 1" | open !| [type /actualType] | eq [::quote/open/SubjectNotQuoteError ::string])
